'''William Stancil''' (born July 16, 1985)<ref>{{Cite web|title=Minnesota Historical Election Archive|url=https://mn.electionarchives.lib.umn.edu/|website=Minnesota Historical Election Archive|access-date=2025-11-30|language=en|first=|last=}}</ref> is an American lawyer, activist, and researcher on housing policy. A [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] and a [[Modern liberalism in the United States|liberal]], Stancil is known for his presence on [[Twitter]]{{efn|name=a}} and [[Bluesky]], where he has defended the [[economic policy of the Biden administration]], criticized [[Operation Metro Surge]], and been frequently harassed by right wing accounts.

In 2024, Stancil ran to represent [[Minnesota's 61A House of Representatives district|District 61A]] in the [[Minnesota House of Representatives]]. During the race, far-right accounts claiming to be Stancil supporters harassed his opponents, who obliquely criticized his use of social media. Stancil was defeated in the district's [[2024 Minnesota House of Representatives election#District 61A|Democratic primary]], earning  36.43% of votes cast.

== Early life and education ==
Stancil was born on July 16, 1985, to a local lawyer and an English teacher in [[Belmont, North Carolina]].<ref name="ballotpedia">{{cite web |title=Will Stancil |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Will_Stancil |website=Ballotpedia |access-date=22 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="intelligencer">{{Cite web|title=Who Is Will Stancil? And Why Is He In Your Feed?|url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/will-stancil-twitter-mn-house-candidate.html|website=Intelligencer|date=2024-04-24|access-date=2025-11-30|language=en|first=David|last=Klion}}</ref> He received a bachelor's degree in history<ref name="linkedin">{{cite web |last1=Stancil |first1=Will |title=Education |url=https://www.linkedin.com/in/will-stancil-62631466/details/education/ |website=LinkedIn |access-date=23 February 2026}}</ref> from [[Wake Forest University]] in 2007 and a master's degree in [[Reconstruction-era]] [[African-American history|Black history]] from [[Queen's University Belfast]] in 2008.<ref name="ballotpedia" /><ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://law.umn.edu/profiles/william-stancil|title=William Stancil '13|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240408233317/https://law.umn.edu/profiles/william-stancil|archive-date=2024-04-08}}</ref><ref>{{cite tweet|first=Will|last=Stancil|user=whstancil|number=1741600042769657944|date=31 December 2023|title=I have a master's degree in reconstruction-era black history|access-date=23 February 2026|url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250621101149/https://x.com/whstancil/status/1741600042769657944 |archive-date=21 June 2025}}</ref> Stancil moved to Minnesota in 2009<ref name="racket" /> and received a juris doctor and master's degree in public policy from the [[University of Minnesota]] in 2013.<ref name="ballotpedia" /><ref name=":0" /> As of 2024, he was a research fellow at the University of Minnesota's Institute of Metropolitan Opportunity, with a focus on civil rights and education and housing policy, working for [[Myron Orfield]], who later endorsed his House campaign.<ref name=":0" /> From 2018 to 2021, Stancil wrote articles for ''[[The Atlantic]]''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Will Stancil|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/author/will-stancil/|website=[[The Atlantic]]|access-date=28 April 2026}}</ref>

== Social media ==
Stancil has been described as one of the "most well-known online pugilists in American politics" by the editor-in-chief of the ''[[States Newsroom|Minnesota Reformer]]''<ref>{{Cite web|title=Will Stancil: 'repetitive and annoying' — and an influential player in American politics|url=https://minnesotareformer.com/2024/02/16/will-stancil-repetitive-and-annoying-and-an-influential-player-in-american-politics/|website=Minnesota Reformer|date=2024-02-16|access-date=2025-11-30|language=en-US|first=J. Patrick|last=Coolican}}</ref> and the [[Minneapolis–Saint Paul]] region's "most prolific [[Intellectual|public intellectual]] on social media" by ''Mpls St.Paul Magazine.''<ref>{{Cite web |last=Platt |first=Adam |date=2025-01-09 |title=In Conversation with Will Stancil |url=https://mspmag.com/api/content/fe98124a-c94c-11ef-a884-12163087a831/ |access-date=2025-12-03 |website=Mpls.St.Paul Magazine |language=en-us}}</ref>

Stancil joined Twitter in 2009, around the time he moved to Minnesota. He stated that he was not particularly active on Twitter until the [[first presidency of Donald Trump]]. Stancil's Twitter account saw a boost in popularity starting in May 2023, when he began posting in favor of the idea of the [[economic policy of the Biden administration]] causing economic growth instead of a recession. This saw positive reception among centrists and negative reception among leftists, the latter group believing that Stancil was a [[shill]] for the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]].<ref name="racket" />

=== Harassment ===
Stancil has been called the "most harassed guy on X" by ''[[Slate (magazine)|Slate]]''.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news|title=The Most Harassed Guy on X Knows What's Wrong With Social Media. Democrats Should Listen to Him.|url=https://slate.com/technology/2025/11/will-stancil-twitter-x-elon-musk-cyberbullying-social-media.html|work=Slate|date=2025-11-06|access-date=2025-11-30|issn=1091-2339|language=en-US|first=Alex|last=Kirshner}}</ref> During his run for Minnesota House of Representatives, far-right accounts further escalated the harassment to his opponents, claiming they were supporters of Stancil.<ref name="newsfromthestates.com">{{Cite web|title=What are the Nazis doing in a south Minneapolis legislative race?|url=https://www.newsfromthestates.com/article/what-are-nazis-doing-south-minneapolis-legislative-race|website=News From The States|access-date=2025-11-30|language=en}}</ref> Such users also prompted [[Grok (chatbot)|Grok]] to tweet graphic fantasies of raping Stancil.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McCoy |first=Joe |date=2025-07-09 |title=AI bot Grok makes disturbing posts about Minneapolis man, who is now mulling legal action |url=https://www.kare11.com/article/tech/x-elon-musk-grok-speech-twitter-ai-artificial-intelligence/89-8dad0222-d8c6-44d9-b07d-686e978ad8ac |access-date=2025-12-03 |website=[[KARE (TV)]] |language=en-US}}</ref> In October 2025, provacateur-cartoonist and self-described Nazi Emily Youcis began posting ''The Will Stancil Show'' on X, an [[Text-to-video model|AI-assisted]] satirical cartoon of Stancil.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Kirshner |first1=Alex |title=The Most Harassed Guy on X Knows What's Wrong With Social Media. Democrats Should Listen to Him. |url=https://slate.com/technology/2025/11/will-stancil-twitter-x-elon-musk-cyberbullying-social-media.html |website=Slate |publisher=The Slate Group |access-date=1 December 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251109155319/https://slate.com/technology/2025/11/will-stancil-twitter-x-elon-musk-cyberbullying-social-media.html |archive-date=9 November 2025 |date=6 November 2025 |quote=In October, a woman with 43,000 followers debuted an A.I.-slop cartoon called “The Will Stancil Show,” depicting a fictional Stancil as a parody of wokeness who hands out free housing vouchers to groups of Black kids.}}</ref><ref name=TAH>{{Cite web|title=The Racist, AI-Generated Future of Entertainment|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/culture/2025/11/will-stancil-show-ai/685058/|website=The Atlantic|date=2025-11-26|access-date=2025-11-30|language=en|first=Tyler Austin|last=Harper}}</ref> The far-right author [[Charles Cornish-Dale]], writing for ''[[The Spectator]]'',  described the series as "better than anything [[Comedy Central]] or [[Adult Swim]] has produced in the last 20 years."<ref>{{Cite web|title=The Will Stancil Show is art|url=https://thespectator.com/topic/the-will-stancil-show-is-art/|website=The Spectator World|date=2025-10-23|access-date=2025-11-30|language=en-US|first=Charles|last=Cornish-Dale}}</ref> By contrast, ''[[The Atlantic]]'' described it as the "racist, AI-generated future of entertainment."<ref name="TAH" />

== 2024 Minnesota House of Representatives campaign ==
{{See also|2024 Minnesota House of Representatives election#District 61A}}
[[File:HD61A DFL Convention Candidates.jpg|thumb|Candidates for endorsement at the SD61 DFL convention, left to right: Trevor Turner, Isabel Rolfes, [[Katie Jones (politician)|Katie Jones]], Dylan McMahon, Jared Brewington, and Will Stancil]]
In 2024, Stancil ran for the [[Minnesota House of Representatives]] in District 61A as part of the [[Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party]]. Stancil ran on a platform of increasing government accountability, revitalizing Minneapolis economically, better public education, enhancing public safety, restoring the tax base, and protecting civil rights.<ref name="reformer knock doors">{{Cite web |last=Griffith |first=Michelle |date=2024-08-02 |title=In Minneapolis House DFL race, candidates knock doors while Twitter looms |url=https://minnesotareformer.com/2024/08/02/in-minneapolis-house-dfl-race-candidates-knock-doors-while-twitter-looms/ |access-date=2025-12-03 |website=Minnesota Reformer |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Five questions with House of Reps 61A candidate Will Stancil |url=https://www.southwestvoices.news/posts/five-questions-with-house-of-reps-61a-candidate-will-stancil |access-date=2025-12-03 |website=www.southwestvoices.news}}</ref>

Far-right accounts claiming to be Stancil supporters harassed his opponents, resulting in his opponents increasing security at public events and limiting their use of Twitter.<ref name="newsfromthestates.com"/> Stancil received veiled criticism from his fellow candidates [[Katie Jones (politician)|Katie Jones]] and Isabel Rolfes for his social media activity, both online and in person.<ref name="reformer knock doors" /> The candidates in the primary agreed that most voters in their district were not aware of Stancil's online presence. Opposition to Stancil mailed various social media posts Stancil had made to voters. Stancil claimed the bulk of online harassment would eventually subside after some weeks.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Albertson-Grove |first1=Josie |title=Internet celebrity colors one state House primary in Minneapolis |url=https://www.startribune.com/internet-celebrity-colors-one-state-house-primary-in-minneapolis/600979353 |access-date=17 February 2026 |language=en |date=9 August 2024}}</ref>

Stancil's endorsements included Carpenters Local 322 and [[Lisa Goodman (politician)|Lisa Goodman]], a former Minneapolis City Councilwoman. His social media presence allowed him to receive $92,000 in campaign funding by August 2024. An independent expenditure committee called United 61A Neighbors, with initial funding of $3,000 from Unidos We Win PAC, was formed with the apparent purpose of causing Stancil to lose the primary.<ref name="reformer knock doors" />

Stancil lost the primary to Katie Jones, who proceeded to win the seat in the general election.<ref>{{cite web |last1=McVan |first1=Madison |title=Results in key Minnesota House and Senate races • Minnesota Reformer |url=https://minnesotareformer.com/2024/08/13/results-in-key-minnesota-house-and-senate-races/ |website=Minnesota Reformer |access-date=17 February 2026 |date=14 August 2024}}</ref>

== Political positions ==
{{Modern liberalism US}}
Stancil's politics are considered [[Centre-left politics|center-left]] or [[Liberal elite|liberal]] and he has received substantial criticism of his positions from both the left and right.<ref name=":3" /><ref>{{Cite web|title=“Go Out There And Be Loud”: An Interview with Will Stancil|url=https://bcgavel.com/2024/03/18/go-out-there-and-be-loud-an-interview-with-will-stancil/|website=The Gavel|date=2024-03-18|access-date=2026-01-31|language=en-US|first=Rob|last=Lee}}</ref> Stancil has been described as being a "die-hard liberal Democrat" since 2003.<ref name="intelligencer" />

Initially holding Republican beliefs in high school, Stancil marched against the [[Iraq War]] in 2002,<ref name="stancil">{{cite web|first=Will|last=Stancil|title=Meet Will|url=https://www.willstancil.org/about1}}</ref> describing it in an interview as "radicalizing" him.<ref name="intelligencer" /> Stancil ran a field office in Belmont for [[Barack Obama 2008 presidential campaign|Barack Obama in 2008]]. In 2020, he supported [[Elizabeth Warren 2020 presidential campaign|Elizabeth Warren's presidential campaign]], and still cites Warren as a good indicator of his basic political outlook.<ref name="intelligencer" /><ref name="stancil" />

Stancil stated in 2024 that he was "scared to death" of [[Donald Trump]] during his first presidency from 2016 to 2020, expressing his wishes that Trump would not be reelected that year.<ref name="reformer knock doors" /> In 2023 and 2024, Stancil argued that the [[Economic policy of the Biden administration|economy under the presidency of Joe Biden]] was successful despite high inflation, claimed that those who argued otherwise were dishonest, and that the negative polarization against Biden was largely as a result of social media.<ref name="racket">{{Cite web|title='I Don't Get Off On It': Meet Will Stancil, the MN Man Caught In a Perpetual Twitter Fight - Racket|url=https://racketmn.com/will-stancil-twitter-who-is-economy-minnesota-minneapolis|website=[[Racket (Minnesota)]]|date=2024-02-02|access-date=2025-11-30|language=en|last=Boller|first=Jay}}</ref>

Stancil believes that voters' perception of the world colored through social media is significantly more important than their material wellbeing, and that psychology was the primary reason [[Kamala Harris]] lost the [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 election]] and [[Trumpism]] is successful.<ref>{{Cite web|title=All that is solid melts into posts: an interview with Will Stancil|url=https://renewal.org.uk/blog/all-that-is-solid-melts-into-posts-an-interview-with-will-stancil/|website=Renewal|date=2025-10-23|access-date=2025-12-01|language=en|last=Jones|first=Morgan}}</ref>

During [[Operation Metro Surge]], Stancil repeatedly tailed [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] agents, and expressed support for the [[Abolish ICE|abolition of ICE]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Warnica|first=Richard|date=2026-01-16|title=Richard Warnica: I spent the week in Minnesota. The only thing I'm sure of now is that more people are going to die|url=https://www.thestar.com/opinion/star-columnists/i-spent-the-week-in-minnesota-the-only-thing-i-m-sure-of-now-is/article_07e4b8b3-b771-426d-bddc-75454d1e847b.html|access-date=2026-01-17|website=Toronto Star|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Welcome to the American Winter|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/2026/01/minneapolis-uprising/685755/|website=The Atlantic|date=2026-01-26|access-date=2026-01-30|language=en|first=Robert F.|last=Worth}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|title=Inside the movement challenging—and disrupting—ICE|url=https://www.economist.com/united-states/2026/01/29/inside-the-movement-challenging-and-disrupting-ice|work=The Economist|access-date=2026-01-31|issn=0013-0613}}</ref> Stancil later claimed that his willingness to take reporters on ridealongs resulted in his removal from [[Signal (software)|Signal]] chat networks in his neighborhood that coordinate direct opposition to ICE agents.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Will Stancil (@whstancil.bsky.social)|url=https://bsky.app/profile/whstancil.bsky.social/post/3mdlz4iwcz22f|work=Bluesky Social|date=2026-01-30|access-date=2026-01-30|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260130075108/https://bsky.app/profile/whstancil.bsky.social/post/3mdlz4iwcz22f|archive-date=January 30, 2026|quote=Okay so unfortunately I’ve been removed from rapid response networks, at least in my immediate area. I think even alerting you of this would be deemed a violation of principles but given how much I’ve talked about it I do feel the need to say what’s up.|url-status=live}}</ref>

